"""Practical recipes; deliberately distinct from the unpublished speech implementation."""
from html import escape

def add(slide,step,node,flow,arrow,link,MODEL,REPO):
 def code(s):return '<pre class="code"><code>'+escape(s)+'</code></pre>'
 def neurons():
  # Native editable SVG: the omitted neurons are explicitly marked.
  cols=[(85,'768 inputs','frozen features'),(440,'128 hidden units','ReLU'),(800,'768 outputs','normalize')]
  s='<svg class="neuron-diagram" viewBox="0 0 1000 220" role="img" aria-label="A trainable MLP maps 768 input coordinates through 128 hidden units to 768 output coordinates; only a few neurons are drawn.">'
  for c,(x,label,sub) in enumerate(cols):
   s+=f'<g data-reveal-step="{min(c+1,3)}">'
   if c:
    for y in [70,115,160]:
     for yy in [70,115,160]:s+=f'<path d="M{cols[c-1][0]+15} {y} L{x-15} {yy}" stroke="#8B5BB5" stroke-width="1" opacity=".3"/>'
   for y in [70,115,160]:s+=f'<circle cx="{x}" cy="{y}" r="12" fill="#FAFAF8" stroke="{ "#3478E5" if not c else "#8B5BB5"}" stroke-width="2"/>'
   s+=f'<text x="{x}" y="25" text-anchor="middle" font-size="22" fill="#30343B">{label}</text><text x="{x}" y="205" text-anchor="middle" font-size="18" fill="#6B7280">{sub}</text><text x="{x+25}" y="153" font-size="30" fill="#6B7280">⋮</text></g>'
  return s+'</svg>'
 slide('index-recipe','Build once: extract → encode → save',
  '<div class="split recipe">'+code('pieces = extract(files)  # text/pages/frames/audio\nX = encode_pieces(pieces)       # (N, 768)\nX = unit_rows(X)                # norm 1 per row\nnp.save("vectors.npy", X)\nsave_json("locations.json", locations)')+step('<div><p class="label blue">One row of metadata per vector</p>'+code('{"file": "lecture.pdf",\n "page": 42,\n "piece_id": "lecture-page-42",\n "model": "google/embeddinggemma-2",\n "revision": "PINNED_REVISION"}')+'</div>',2)+'</div>'+step('<p class="plain-note">Keep row order identical in the vector file and metadata file.</p>',3),
  'Start with NumPy + JSON. A database becomes useful as the collection grows.',
  '<p>This is a teaching skeleton, not DigUp source. extract, encode_pieces and save_json are application functions: extraction supplies text passages, decoded images, audio windows or video frames; encoding calls the model’s modality-specific adapter. They are named abstractions, not claimed library APIs. Cache model revision, preprocessing settings and an input-content hash so changed content can be re-embedded. Metadata values above are illustrative.</p><p>For text, the official model card uses SentenceTransformer("google/embeddinggemma-2"), model.encode(documents, prompt_name="Document", normalize_embeddings=True). Queries use SearchQuery. Images/audio are sent through their media input paths without text task prefixes. Use the same model, revision, dimensions and compatible preprocessing for index and query. See PRACTICAL.md for a concrete text-only index script and the contract for adding other modalities.</p>',
  link(MODEL,'Encoding API')+' · '+link('PRACTICAL.md','Build notes & runnable scripts'),
  ['Encode and cache the pieces','Save the source locations beside them','Keep the cache consistent'], 'TEACHING IMPLEMENTATION · extraction and media encoding are explicit adapter steps')
 slide('lookup-recipe','At query time: encode → score → look up the source',
  code('X = np.load("vectors.npy")        # (N, 768), already normalized\nq = encode_query(question)        # (768,)\nq = q / np.linalg.norm(q)\nscores = X @ q                    # (N,), one cosine per piece\nbest = np.argsort(-scores)[:5]\nfor row in best:\n    print(scores[row], locations[row])')+step('<div class="cache-note"><b>No re-encoding the whole collection for every question.</b><span>Edit a file → refresh its pieces. Change the embedding model → rebuild the compatible index.</span></div>',2),
  'For a small course collection, exact matrix multiplication is enough to begin.',
  '<p>This is the semantic baseline. The query helper in the companion script calls the official SearchQuery prompt. Keep raw cosine scores; they are not confidence probabilities. A production system can add DigUp’s lexical index, calibration and file grouping. For larger corpora, an approximate-nearest-neighbor index trades some recall for search cost, but it is unnecessary for the first small example.</p><p>Check whether the correct source is in the top five for questions you wrote before tuning. Include keyword-sensitive examples and unanswerable questions. A stored location must be valid even if its semantic score is high. The companion script runs on text pieces; a media extractor must be implemented before claiming this searches an arbitrary folder.</p>',
  link(REPO,'DigUp implementation')+' · '+link('code/semantic_search.py','Minimal Python search'),
  ['Look up the nearest stored vectors','Decide when the index needs refreshing'], 'TEACHING IMPLEMENTATION · semantic lookup baseline, before hybrid ranking')
 slide('training-data','First build a small, labelled word-recognition task',
  '<div class="data-example"><div><p class="label teal">INPUT</p><p class="lead">A short audio clip</p><p>mono · 16 kHz<br>one spoken command</p></div>'+arrow()+'<div><p class="label purple">TARGET</p><p class="lead">“yes”</p><p>integer label: 0<br>one label per clip</p></div></div>'+step('<p class="dataset-line"><b>Speech Commands v0.02:</b> use yes, no, up, down, left, right, stop, go.</p>',2)+step('<p class="plain-note">Use the official train / validation / test lists; keep speakers separate.<br>Cache an N × 768 feature matrix and N integer labels for each split.</p>',3),
  'This is an eight-word teaching experiment. It is not yet full speech-to-text.',
  '<p>Proposed reproducible teaching dataset, not the dataset claimed for Shahir’s reader. Speech Commands v0.02 contains short labelled spoken-word WAVs. Select the eight named word directories and retain the official validation/testing lists; other eligible files are training data. The speaker identifier before _nohash_ enables an explicit leakage check. Clips may be shorter than one second: preprocess consistently as mono 16 kHz and keep a record of any padding. Do not mix cropped portions of one recording across splits.</p><p>Compute one native 768D normalized EmbeddingGemma 2 output for each clip, with the model frozen. These semantic embeddings may lose phonetic distinctions, so success is an experiment to measure, not a promised result. Eight-way word accuracy is the appropriate first metric; it is not sentence-level WER.</p>',
  link('https://arxiv.org/abs/1804.03209','Speech Commands')+' · '+link('https://github.com/pytorch/audio/blob/main/src/torchaudio/datasets/speechcommands.py','Official split handling')+' · '+link('PRACTICAL.md','Data preparation'),
  ['Pair one clip with one label','Choose a small vocabulary','Split before caching the features'], 'PROPOSED TEACHING DATASET · not a reconstruction of the author’s training corpus')
 slide('reader-shapes','A 768-dimensional vector is 768 input features',
  neurons()+step('<div class="shape-chain"><span>X: <b>B × 768</b></span>'+arrow()+'<span>hidden: <b>B × 128</b></span>'+arrow()+'<span>R: <b>B × 768</b></span></div>',3)+step('<p class="calculation">R Eᵀ / τ → <b>B × 8 word scores</b></p><p class="plain-note center">E stores eight frozen text vectors: 8 × 768. Here τ = 0.1 is a chosen hyperparameter.</p>',4),
  'Train the small mapping. Keep the embedding model and candidate word vectors fixed.',
  '<p>Architecture chosen for this classroom experiment: Linear(768,128), ReLU, Linear(128,768), then L2 normalization. B is minibatch size. Each input coordinate is one feature; the drawing shows only three neurons from each layer and marks the omitted ones. The two linear layers have 197,504 trainable parameters including biases. Candidate words are encoded with the frozen text branch using a consistent Classification prompt and normalized. The matrix E is held fixed.</p><p>Multiplying normalized reader outputs by E transpose supplies cosine-based logits, divided by a positive temperature. A simpler Linear(768,8) classifier is also a valid baseline. This slide intentionally illustrates a text-vector readout to connect to CLIP and the author’s nearest-word idea. The author’s actual reader input dimensions and layers are not disclosed.</p>',
  link(MODEL,'Native 768D output')+' · '+link('code/train_reader.py','Runnable cached-feature trainer'),
  ['Read 768 coordinates as features','Mix them with a small hidden layer','Map back into the comparison space','Compare against eight candidate word vectors'], 'PROPOSED TEACHING READER · 768 → 128 → 768; not the published app’s architecture')
 slide('reader-loss','The correct word supplies the training target',
  '<div class="loss-example"><p class="lead">Audio says <b>“yes”</b> → target y = 0</p><table class="numbers"><thead><tr><th>Candidate word</th><th>yes</th><th>no</th><th>up</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th>Illustrative softmax share</th><td class="score">0.70</td><td>0.20</td><td>0.10</td></tr></tbody></table></div>'+step('<p class="loss-formula">L = − log p(correct word) = − log 0.70 ≈ <b>0.357</b></p>',2)+step('<div class="update-path">loss → gradients → reader weights <span>· frozen encoder and word bank stay fixed</span></div>',3),
  'Average this loss over the minibatch, update the reader, then evaluate on held-out speakers.',
  '<p>Three candidate words are shown only to make the arithmetic small; the proposed experiment has eight. Probabilities are illustrative and do not come from a trained model. With logits S = normalize(reader(X)) Eᵀ / tau and labels y, use cross_entropy(S, y). PyTorch performs log-softmax internally, so do not pass already-softmaxed probabilities to cross_entropy. The default mean reduction averages the minibatch.</p><p>This cross-entropy is our explicitly chosen word-classification objective. It is not claimed to be Shahir’s unpublished reader loss. Only reader parameters are in the optimizer. For head-only training, cached embeddings detach the base model entirely. Finetuning the encoder instead requires audio inputs, gradients through the model, a new optimizer scope, and rebuilding any affected index.</p>',
  link('code/train_reader.py','Teaching objective in code')+' · '+link('https://docs.pytorch.org/docs/stable/generated/torch.nn.functional.cross_entropy.html','PyTorch cross-entropy'),
  ['Identify the labelled word','Calculate its negative log probability','Send the gradient only to the reader'], 'ILLUSTRATIVE PROBABILITIES · chosen training objective for our small experiment')
 slide('reader-code','The training step fits on one slide',
  code('reader = nn.Sequential(nn.Linear(768, 128), nn.ReLU(),\n                       nn.Linear(128, 768))\noptimizer = torch.optim.AdamW(reader.parameters(), lr=1e-3)\n\nfor X, y in loader:                    # (B, 768), (B,)\n    R = F.normalize(reader(X), dim=-1) # (B, 768)\n    logits = R @ E.T / 0.1             # E: (8, 768); logits: (B, 8)\n    loss = F.cross_entropy(logits, y)  # mean across the minibatch\n    optimizer.zero_grad()\n    loss.backward()\n    optimizer.step()'),
  'X is a cached embedding batch. y is a batch of word labels. E is a frozen word-vector table.',
  '<p>Imports, validated cache loading, shuffling, epochs and validation are in code/train_reader.py. The code is a teaching reader, not the speech app’s source. The runnable companion reports validation accuracy and selects the best validation epoch; test evaluation is separate. It accepts actual cached 768D features and does not generate fake embeddings if the cache is absent. The shape-and-gradient check uses random tensors and does not measure model quality.</p><p>Compare this reader with the zero-shot baseline X Eᵀ on the same held-out speakers. Better training loss alone is insufficient. The proposed learning rate, hidden width and temperature are starting choices to tune only on validation data.</p>',
  link('code/train_reader.py','Complete cached-feature trainer')+' · '+link('PRACTICAL.md','Input/output contract'),
  None, 'TEACHING CODE · cached real features required; model training results are not claimed')
 slide('full-transcription','Full transcription adds an alignment problem',
  '<div class="data-example"><div><p class="label teal">INPUT</p><p class="lead">An entire utterance</p><p>many words, different durations</p></div>'+arrow()+'<div><p class="label purple">TARGET</p><p class="lead">“please turn left”</p><p>an ordered sequence of words</p></div></div>'+step('<div class="split alignment-options"><div><p class="label blue">A practical dataset</p><p>LibriSpeech: paired English speech and transcripts.<br>Start with train-clean-100.</p></div><div><p class="label purple">An extra design decision</p><p>Keep time-indexed features.<br>Learn alignment, or obtain word timings before training.</p></div></div>',2)+step('<p class="plain-note">Validate on dev-clean; reserve test-clean / test-other for final WER.<br>Keyword accuracy does not establish sentence transcription quality.</p>',3),
  'One pooled 768D vector per recording does not preserve a usable word-by-word timeline.',
  '<p>LibriSpeech is a proposed next dataset, not a verified training source for Shahir’s model. It provides 16 kHz read English audio and transcripts under CC BY 4.0. The author names only openly licensed English speech and reports LibriSpeech evaluation. Do not convert that evaluation fact into a claim about their training corpus.</p><p>Two practical designs: keep a temporal feature sequence and train with an alignment-aware sequence objective such as CTC; or obtain word timings using an external forced aligner, train on aligned segments, and design segmentation/merging for inference. These are proposed options, not claims about the original implementation. A faithful reproduction would require the reader architecture, feature tap, objective, corpus, vocabulary and decoding details from the author. Score final transcripts by WER and measure end-to-end runtime on the same recordings.</p>',
  link('https://www.openslr.org/12','LibriSpeech dataset & splits')+' · '+link('PRACTICAL.md','Going beyond isolated words'),
  ['Change the output from one label to a sequence','Choose the data and solve alignment','Evaluate words and timing'], 'PRACTICAL NEXT STEP · proposed route to ASR; author’s exact training recipe is not public')
